   The biggest rule in Usenetiquette is the proper use of quoting.  Each
and every Usenet post should contain its own context, especially if
it's a reply to a previous post.  For example, as I answer your questions,
I am quoting the questions above my replies.  That way, a new reader can
start reading in the middle of the conversation without much difficulty;
he'll see what happened immediately before to provoke my response.
   Quotes should be properly attributed: "Mumon wrote:" is the bare 
minimum.  Some people add to that attribution line things like:
     * The newsgroup in which the post was read (useful for replying
       to widely crossposted messages).
     * The Message-ID of the quoted post (not useful at all, since that
       same information is repeated in the message headers).
     * The From: header of the quoted post (useful if it's a valid
       e-mail address and someone wants to contact the OP, but it hardly
       ever is, these days, and private e-mail about a newsgroup thread
       is a pretty rare occurrence anyway).
     * The timestamp of the quoted post (useful in that it might discourage
       replies to month-old posts --- a serious problem now that Google
       Groups Beta is around).
   And, of course, quotes should be accurate.  Spellchecking (as 
"their-->[there]" in the quote above) is somewhat widespread, and it's 
considered actually /rude/ not to snip the parts of a post to which
you're not responding; but other than that, obviously, quotes should be
accurate.  (Lest you be answered with a curt "That's not what I said!")

> Second what rules is Google breaking (such as exactly what is it doing
> incorrectly with the line wrapping)

   What rules /isn't/ Google Groups Beta breaking?  Its default "Reply"
button (the one visible at the bottom of each post) doesn't include quoted 
context in the reply window, so it encourages bad quoting netiquette
there.  It censors certain sequences of characters, such as 
email@addresses, which violates the Prime Directive of Usenet: accurate 
quoting.  It also does horrible things with line-wrapping and quoting;
I've seen before, but not verified recently, that a line such as
From: foo@bar.com
will give Google Groups Beta trouble.
   Most importantly from a Usenetter's perspective, though, IMO, is that
Google Groups Beta is basically a tool for deluging Usenet with a bunch
of clueless newbies who don't know netiquette and don't care to figure
it out, because Google doesn't give them any hints.  Cf. "endless 
September."  And it's a feedback loop: the more newbies post incorrectly,
the more incorrect posts there are in Google's archive for said newbies
to point to and go, "Well, skriptkyd082 did it, so it must be right!"
(Or: "Well, Google did it, and they must know something about Usenet!")

   Another serious problem is Google's insistence on commingling Usenet
and Google's own AOL-style chatrooms in its Beta interface.  Real users
have a hard time filtering out the "noise" of the proprietary chatrooms,
and newbies often get confused as to what belongs to Google and what
belongs to everyone (via Usenet).  I've even seen messages (originating
within Google Groups Beta) crossposted between a Google proprietary
group and a Usenet group, with the result that readers in the Real
World can't reply except by removing the crosspost (since Usenet servers
in the World Outside don't, and probably /can't/ legally, carry Google's 
proprietary stuff).

